<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Berkeley</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1685">1685</a>–1753</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Bishop Berkeley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Berkeley was an Irish philosopher and Anglican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Cloyne, educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin">Trinity College Dublin</a>. In works such as A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710) he argued for immaterialism, the view that physical objects exist only as perceived. He also wrote on vision, mathematics and, in The Querist, on the economic problems of Ireland.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-berkeley.jpg" alt="George Berkeley" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Smibert · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Smibert_-_Bishop_George_Berkeley_-_Google_Art_Project_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Protestant Ireland produced an intellectual culture of its own: Trinity College Dublin was founded in 1592, and Jonathan Swift, <b>George Berkeley</b>, Oliver Goldsmith and Edmund Burke were among its writers and thinkers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dysart Castle, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.5,-7.12&amp;year=1729" aria-label="Dysart Castle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4390 352 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of George Berkeley"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4840 622h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4840 622h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.5,-7.12&amp;year=1729">What was here in 1729? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Their ESSE is PERCIPI, nor is it possible they should have any existence out of the minds or thinking things which perceive them.&#8221;</p><footer>A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710), section 3 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4723/pg4723-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…the far greater part, if not all, of those difficulties which have hitherto amused philosophers, and blocked up the way to knowledge, are entirely owing to ourselves--that we have first raised a dust and then complain we cannot see.&#8221;</p><footer>A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710), introduction &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4723/pg4723-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…all the choir of heaven and furniture of the earth, in a word all those bodies which compose the mighty frame of the world, have not any subsistence without a mind…&#8221;</p><footer>A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge (1710), section 6 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4723/pg4723-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
